every natural number is also an integer number, $\\sqrt{121}$ also belongs to $Z.$ \n\nOn the other hand, the sets ${Z}^{-}$ and ${Q}^{-}$ only contain negative numbers. Therefore, $\\sqrt{121}$ does not belong to either of these sets. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Polynomials is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048572 :topic/id #uuid "aabe8b8c-a429-5abb-824a-40f9fdf376e0" :topic/math-academy-id 2104 :topic/title "Orthogonal Sets in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1ab2831-9a65-5a58-9e8a-cb5dbbe7e1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461335} {:answer/id #uuid "0ddb2640-9d14-5cc8-8e8d-4e974dd74693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ddb2640-9d14-5cc8-8e8d-4e974dd74693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461336} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cb8992c-bc36-52ec-a230-f17c730b784a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462421} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c38c193-c193-5906-bff7-21c4434a17d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461337} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b751a5-d9a2-52fd-83eb-a65b524c7c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7b751a5-d9a2-52fd-83eb-a65b524c7c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461338} :answer-field/id #uuid "9da2ecac-8cc7-5c9c-ad53-6433a655dfdb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462422} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10524738-640c-51dc-a18a-a31d95b37711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461339} {:answer/id #uuid "1885443b-c221-5703-9e91-8edc29b40e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1885443b-c221-5703-9e91-8edc29b40e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461340} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bbd1dcf-5a8c-5d7c-aec6-1f3c9e1d54eb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "95a9cd65-803f-5d19-875e-716631b2f8a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104734" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}+t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}-t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)={t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $2$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **II.** ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **III.** $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}+t,{t}^{2}-t⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{-1}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}\\,+\\,(-1))\\,\\cdot \\,((-1)^{2}\\,-\\,(-1))\\,+\\,({0}^{2}\\,+\\,0)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,0)\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,+\\,1)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}+t,{t}^{2}-1⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{-1}\\,+\\,({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}\\,+\\,(-1))\\,\\cdot \\,((-1)^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({0}^{2}\\,+\\,0)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,+\\,1)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Let's check if ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}-t,{t}^{2}-1⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{-1}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}\\,-\\,(-1))\\,\\cdot \\,((-1)^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,0)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, $S$ is an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Polynomials is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048572 :topic/id #uuid "aabe8b8c-a429-5abb-824a-40f9fdf376e0" :topic/math-academy-id 2104 :topic/title "Orthogonal Sets in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41074944-190b-5200-8ca5-70749fdf342e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461341} {:answer/id #uuid "c22ad1f9-915d-5c7b-a895-be73f39a5ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c22ad1f9-915d-5c7b-a895-be73f39a5ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461342} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e0c8223-726b-5e18-814f-2ce6bb661414" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462424} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8308e810-9158-545a-bda0-776e2aee683b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461343} {:answer/id #uuid "83b9fc92-e7ac-5745-a0e4-64d5dea39c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83b9fc92-e7ac-5745-a0e4-64d5dea39c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461344} :answer-field/id #uuid "06a0f42f-f241-5187-8256-6b0cadab396d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462425} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fafe3ff7-e1fb-5eb9-b544-a83e81be0784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461345} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0dc6d8-7ec7-53ad-9eb2-5fdd1f6ccd7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fafe3ff7-e1fb-5eb9-b544-a83e81be0784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461345} :answer-field/id #uuid "49440439-3ff0-5069-8892-56fdd4d6fa07" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d2cd958-2383-51a8-8ef7-f3c1ff9a5b43" :question/math-academy-id "q-104736" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}-4,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}-t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)={t}^{2}-2t.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $2$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **II.** ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **III.** $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}-4,{t}^{2}-t⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{2}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,0)\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,2(2)) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}-t,{t}^{2}-2t⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{1}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{2}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\,-\\,0)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,2(0))\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,2(1))\\,+\\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,2)\\,\\cdot \\,({2}^{2}-2(2)) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}-4,{t}^{2}-2t⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{1}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{2}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,2(0))\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,2(1))\\,+\\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,2(2)) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172031 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050312 :topic/id #uuid "3efe4c7e-e297-5f41-9d4f-bfd04820c753" :topic/math-academy-id 3334 :topic/title "Sets and Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b744b62f-7f22-53a7-b5da-e0e0c4b71ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461347} {:answer/id #uuid "92814613-8a79-5173-923d-9ad1cc3a7dce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b744b62f-7f22-53a7-b5da-e0e0c4b71ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461347} :answer-field/id #uuid "43842a60-07cb-5ebf-bbc0-158d369bd524" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462427} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16cf430f-f840-51c7-b3a5-49fc2b352ec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461349} {:answer/id #uuid "290199d5-5b07-5cb3-b686-77f3e250cf17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "290199d5-5b07-5cb3-b686-77f3e250cf17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461350} :answer-field/id #uuid "f49d7363-5cf4-50c1-a5bc-9d2b72b9f967" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462428} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3716543-54eb-5b3c-9127-6173d7248e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461351} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8ffded-e404-508f-bfb3-fdbde11b4ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3716543-54eb-5b3c-9127-6173d7248e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461351} :answer-field/id #uuid "7208be36-56ce-5f88-8abd-e54f69250567" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f571336-e197-53b2-942f-7691710c5fd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104778" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the codomain ${0,1}?$\n- **I.** $p:{0,1}\\to Z$\n- **II.** $q:[0,1]\\to {0,1}$\n- **III.** $r:{0,1}\\to (0,1)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $Z,$ the set of integers.\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is ${0,1},$ the set containing the two numbers $0$ and $1$ only.\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is $(0,1),$ the interval from $0$ to $1$ (excluding both $0$ and $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172031 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050312 :topic/id #uuid "3efe4c7e-e297-5f41-9d4f-bfd04820c753" :topic/math-academy-id 3334 :topic/title "Sets and Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7c7cf4d-3a38-5af6-9cc5-fe045e956d14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461353} {:answer/id #uuid "75fb4840-8bbe-5eed-b96d-339109e3b5f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75fb4840-8bbe-5eed-b96d-339109e3b5f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461354} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a676279-8bc4-5a9f-9ca6-ac1faad98477" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462430} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00808ca8-a336-53a4-9ff9-9c6d4b225a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461355} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e6a0d9-b48c-550e-aea7-fe84d05858f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00808ca8-a336-53a4-9ff9-9c6d4b225a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461355} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1954802-0efd-5399-b549-0601aaea7a2b" 