447883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e8c8441-a2c1-5d8b-ac9b-5c23beabb8c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447882} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c426514-3859-57fd-a486-bab9ad75ab38" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38344b2a-4c7e-5844-831c-94e852b34e5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-107131" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${0,1,t}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{2}[t]$\n- **II.** $2-t$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${0,1,t}$\n- **III.** ${0,1,t}$ is a basis of ${R}_{2}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1+0\\cdot t=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n2-t=0\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot t.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048819 :topic/id #uuid "c29ac9c3-f2c1-572b-9251-f82101c5a30e" :topic/math-academy-id 1375 :topic/title "The Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d58913e0-0a2e-503b-895a-9e8a62183598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447884} {:answer/id #uuid "431ce8d2-3792-5889-8d11-0ad0193ca84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "431ce8d2-3792-5889-8d11-0ad0193ca84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447885} :answer-field/id #uuid "f788e45d-0eb5-52b3-a555-a20ba4a7e688" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448982} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "246df31b-44fb-5288-810d-e408e57f7633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447886} {:answer/id #uuid "9d8cf59f-c8d2-5114-8a53-9044ab7758d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "246df31b-44fb-5288-810d-e408e57f7633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447886} :answer-field/id #uuid "485057f3-721a-5540-bdfe-9c71b7bee298" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448983} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7efe3c0-c40d-5ad1-93ce-b169cedbc81c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447888} {:answer/id #uuid "89361e74-53be-59dc-b7e9-2d650cec0404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89361e74-53be-59dc-b7e9-2d650cec0404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447889} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3e25578-3be1-5e27-a25b-8070d738ce2d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eace6f7a-724d-598c-b716-132ee8202185" :question/math-academy-id "q-107137" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{5}.$\n- **II.** An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =6.$\n- **III.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $7v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=5v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=5{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $5$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=\\frac{1}{5}v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{6}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(7v)=7Av=7(2v)=2(7v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(7v)=2(7v),$ and $7v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $7v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050453 :topic/id #uuid "4a436386-bf41-55c7-b1d3-02adad8f3faf" :topic/math-academy-id 1907 :topic/title "Subspaces of Abstract Vector Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "083e9208-bad4-5d95-8617-a808610cfd9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447890} {:answer/id #uuid "e82608a8-5685-52b5-b9cd-f37053507f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e82608a8-5685-52b5-b9cd-f37053507f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447891} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae9dedb2-72df-5b77-917f-220b9a4e4d60" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448985} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8db24278-c96a-5a68-af63-c6a6368e9a8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447892} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd30e1e-5645-5eaa-adf3-d528528c397e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbd30e1e-5645-5eaa-adf3-d528528c397e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447893} :answer-field/id #uuid "5606dc3a-9849-5dfb-b18a-a4377b645866" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448986} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74947044-d3b9-5c81-bb3b-1786caec1ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447894} {:answer/id #uuid "61b2ff36-6eee-584b-a567-1e35d3ce9ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61b2ff36-6eee-584b-a567-1e35d3ce9ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447895} :answer-field/id #uuid "fff9a238-82c2-5126-9f41-aac7f75ee83b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448987} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74a73409-a50b-5846-a8bf-f9fe458b594e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447896} {:answer/id #uuid "ec914062-c912-54b4-af4b-477d6aaf73ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74a73409-a50b-5846-a8bf-f9fe458b594e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447896} :answer-field/id #uuid "948b0716-db3d-59c5-a456-3c63914b24c4" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7a7e364-e908-54cb-a453-163231b198f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-107139" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $N$ containing all functions such that $f(0)=0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $N$ is closed under function addition\n- **II.** $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $N$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- **IV.** $N$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, suppose $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are functions from $N.$ Then $f(x)=g(x)=0$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(0) & =f(0)+g(0) \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $N$ is closed under function addition.\n\n- Statement II is true.  Indeed, suppose $f(x)\\in N$  and let $\\alpha \\in R.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha f)(0) & =\\alpha \\cdot f(0) \\\\  & =\\alpha \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $N$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050453 :topic/id #uuid "4a436386-bf41-55c7-b1d3-02adad8f3faf" :topic/math-academy-id 1907 :topic/title "Subspaces of Abstract Vector Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8e680db-ef52-5417-81b7-6ffe9b4383d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447898} {:answer/id #uuid "19085411-2145-5329-9d1c-475729adfa8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8e680db-ef52-5417-81b7-6ffe9b4383d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447898} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b1197ba-c177-572c-bb95-f33fa8ad5099" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448989} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82cf67a9-3d8f-5ef6-a855-bb498bf43588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447900} {:answer/id #uuid "ae64a3e8-8b17-5d18-800a-c83f9d6df476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82cf67a9-3d8f-5ef6-a855-bb498bf43588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447900} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3c1736b-4604-5446-818c-f015abcb2042" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448990} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99d1af0d-a2b6-573e-97da-18a0ba798138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447902} {:answer/id #uuid "0672ccdf-f372-53ce-8a7f-559b6aa2d02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99d1af0d-a2b6-573e-97da-18a0ba798138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447902} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee0fabf2-df84-53f3-93cc-284ded8c1cc6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448991} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07c2fad8-37dd-5870-9bbb-1b36235bf7bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447904} {:answer/id #uuid "164c2063-3e52-529b-8591-4a7fddde2578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "164c2063-3e52-529b-8591-4a7fddde2578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447905} :answer-field/id #uuid "2108e5a4-e8f2-5a2c-9c41-5db4f1dcbb80" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a3ebe5cf-3b69-5829-b6a6-d075f8831709" :question/math-academy-id "q-107141" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $W$ containing all polynomials of degree greater than $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $W$ is closed under function addition\n- **II.** $W$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $W$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- **IV.** $W$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}+1$ and $g(x)=-{x}^{3}+1,$ then\n\n$$\nf(x)+g(x)={x}^{3}+1+(-{x}^{3})+1=2,\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial of degree zero. So, $W$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot {x}^{3}=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $W$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $W$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050453 :topic/id #uuid "4a436386-bf41-55c7-b1d3-02adad8f3faf" :topic/math-academy-id 1907 :topic/title "Subspaces of Abstract Vector Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51114197-48b4-5104-bfb9-1476694d96da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447906} {:answer/id #uuid "76b7ee44-2461-590b-9754-2f59b1831440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76b7ee44-2461-590b-9754-2f59b1831440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447907} :answer-field/id #uuid "228f45e6-c374-52fd-9af8-a8ca562b3eb7" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448993} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cc0ede9-e1c1-5c90-b6a3-9be6bdd5c841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447908} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3441cf-7ed5-523d-a20d-91318604e063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cc0ede9-e1c1-5c90-b6a3-9be6bdd5c841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447908} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d23d1f2-1c32-549b-9978-90943734579d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448994} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff373b9d-e811-514f-bce5-161bb2f488cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447910} {:answer/id #uuid "d7052237-5175-51f8-9c78-f0a3feb94b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff373b9d-e811-514f-bce5-161bb2f488cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447910} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b22ca3-cfec-5809-bb74-b76f5be4a5f7" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448995} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d876b16-10bc-5bc7-9b09-7e7c24f0338d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447912} {:answer/id #uuid "70479fdf-b7a2-500e-afb0-203ef2537fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70479fdf-b7a2-500e-afb0-203ef2537fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447913} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3395aee-c072-53c7-a7f4-02a2e6729e0a" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "997e8053-09a0-5e0a-853a-5f7c609b31ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-107143" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $M$ containing all functions that return an integer value at $x=1.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $M$ is closed under function addition\n- **II.** $M$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $M$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- **IV.** $M$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, if $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are functions from $M,$ then $f(1)$  and  $g(1)$ are integers and so $(f+g)(1)=f(1)+g(1)$ must be an integer number too. So, $M$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, $f(x)=1$ is a function in $M$, but $0.5f(x)$ is not in $M$ because\n$(0.5f)(1)=0.5\\cdot f(1)=0.5$ is not an integer. So, $M$ is *not*  closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $M$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048819 :topic/id #uuid "c29ac9c3-f2c1-572b-9251-f82101c5a30e" :topic/math-academy-id 1375 :topic/title "The Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa87efff-4a12-56eb-a8fd-30204b0128fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447914} {:answer/id #uuid "4674332e-4632-5d4c-b6e9-889347caedd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4674332e-4632-5d4c-b6e9-889347caedd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447915} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee73d125-3aa6-5c82-880d-64be3daa5022" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448997} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d240ce2-a819-5de6-9ea8-364883e337ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447916} {:answer/id #uuid "fdeef0e6-f504-51f6-b05a-04420b84e548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d240ce2-a819-5de6-9ea8-364883e337ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447916} :answer-field/id #uuid "658234fd-f35d-56ed-9746-688865f1db64" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448998} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a78808fb-0db9-59dc-916a-c7ff9581c064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447918} {:answer/id #uuid "57744455-0207-5c3b-80ce-66fb801d7ec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57744455-0207-5c3b-80ce-66fb801d7ec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447919} :answer-field/id #uuid "4063c812-d499-5f4b-adec-12943b0570ef" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "580e535d-24ca-5116-ba35-64ccc5c2ef28" :question/math-academy-id "q-107314" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{7}$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =7.$\n- **II.** An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =1.$\n- **III.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2},$ then $-v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=\\frac{1}{7}v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{7}{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $7$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=7v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $7.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(\\frac{1}{2}v)=\\frac{1}{2}(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=\\frac{1}{2}(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244046 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552 :topic/id #uuid "a92f5097-81a3-51a2-90e4-397763d967f0" :topic/math-academy-id 1909 :topic/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd6a732f-6cdd-5655-a6d6-50c4868da54c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447920} {:answer/id #uuid "5fbf8f5f-a4a5-551f-94f1-dcb9e98cce3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd6a732f-6cdd-5655-a6d6-50c4868da54c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447920} :answer-field/id #uuid "7be9815c-003c-59b0-bf27-d84a02bc7230" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449000} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28275147-4f3e-5c35-a4b7-26449bd54feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447922} {:answer/id #uuid "4df74a3a-11fc-54e3-9232-cbadde994cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4df74a3a-11fc-54e3-9232-cbadde994cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447923} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a7adf00-543a-53ac-bd3f-338d162d18d1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449001} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07492db5-983b-53e0-8d42-a96b1163f4df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447924} {:answer/id #uuid "7100bd53-8f94-5723-9f27-437f2039702e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07492db5-983b-53e0-8d42-a96b1163f4df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447924} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5f0bf28-1c7e-5b15-aea9-897d2c7a4160" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "265a681e-3e0d-553b-975a-40472b8deb57" :question/math-academy-id "q-107347" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- **II.** $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- **III.** ${\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-matrix is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=0\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+(-3)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552 :topic/id #uuid "a92f5097-81a3-51a2-90e4-397763d967f0" :topic/math-academy-id 1909 :topic/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces"} :activity/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485759} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486493}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552 :topic/id #uuid "a92f5097-81a3-51a2-90e4-397763d967f0" :topic/math-academy-id 1909 :topic/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c503b3e-b3ee-58f2-b568-8feac45ab65f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447926} {:answer/id #uuid "18ceae87-0b3a-552a-904a-8aa94f6b3988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18ceae87-0b3a-552a-904a-8aa94f6b3988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447927} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b525ee4-3cf7-5d76-a53d-685b40668c88" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449003} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "784695a4-512d-5fdd-8693-62a2ab3a9b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447928} {:answer/id #uuid "cf7d3d75-f754-5646-a945-d3f52334331d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf7d3d75-f754-5646-a945-d3f52334331d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447929} :answer-field/id #uuid "028c41b0-ebc3-5901-b580-98c12b660e74" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449004} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "487f3bbf-a084-5508-9f38-062284a461b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447930} {:answer/id #uuid "2def3e0c-88ec-541d-aa1e-fc1120e9cd1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "487f3bbf-a084-5508-9f38-062284a461b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447930} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e422815-847c-5334-a438-2686d5ae65a2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "727e9706-00b1-50a3-b21c-2eb79073afed" :question/math-academy-id "q-107534" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- **II.** $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- **III.** ${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{21}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+b\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & -a \\\\ 0 & 2b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552 :topic/id #uuid "a92f5097-81a3-51a2-90e4-397763d967f0" :topic/math-academy-id 1909 :topic/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces"} :activity/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485759} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486497}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552 :topic/id #uuid "a92f5097-81a3-51a2-90e4-397763d967f0" :topic/math-academy-id 1909 :topic/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "308a8ed8-8d44-58b9-aaf1-e33979b6d166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447932} {:answer/id #uuid "958d6e8a-0fac-5601-9779-a8576a42bcc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "308a8ed8-8d44-58b9-aaf1-e33979b6d166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447932} :answer-field/id #uuid "d732d5d9-f30e-58c7-83a6-3a8d5892d490" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449006} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cd517ee-8673-500f-afa1-eacdcea416ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447934} {:answer/id #uuid "de76f5a0-c339-57f4-bd81-cfd33d090fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de76f5a0-c339-57f4-bd81-cfd33d090fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447935} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c241e3e-a093-58cd-84f4-dc30c59df211" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449007} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b3623b7-9257-514c-a794-738ea4d98ff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447936} {:answer/id #uuid "93d8002f-68fd-5bd3-bbf4-5523386f1e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b3623b7-9257-514c-a794-738ea4d98ff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447936} :answer-field/id #uuid "034309db-1b50-5a7d-a770-c53c3d718529" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3a9f3d6-7047-5a33-b81e-ab3641786305" :question/math-academy-id "q-107536" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- **II.** $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- **III.** ${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere some coefficients are nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}=1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552 :topic/id #uuid "a92f5097-81a3-51a2-90e4-397763d967f0" :topic/math-academy-id 1909 :topic/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "605acb7f-0c8c-50ed-ab0c-0e1db5013edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447938} {:answer/id #uuid "657b255a-5461-5921-9c09-1dfc9a49f297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "605acb7f-0c8c-50ed-ab0c-0e1db5013edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447938} :answer-field/id #uuid "201145fb-e785-5042-9cad-9304f8fd4c49" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449009} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce65d0a2-bcf1-5386-80c6-bae2988afc8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447940} {:answer/id #uuid "87304bb0-dbab-5188-af76-2353aaf3b331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce65d0a2-bcf1-5386-80c6-bae2988afc8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447940} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec75e391-e956-5ba8-93b0-974ba31d1b28" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449010} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdb24fab-b924-5594-acf5-3145248ec006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447942} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f09d35-1adc-5fc8-b63a-eba17838500d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5f09d35-1adc-5fc8-b63a-eba17838500d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447943} :answer-field/id #uuid "c92d836a-ca99-5b03-92e4-d7ddb6e6d591" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d745992-cf32-533c-af78-280d0db594aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-107587" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n\n- **II.** $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n\n- **III.** $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains a vector from another vector space.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{2}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}({c}_{1}+{c}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}){c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $C$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3}=({x}_{1}-{x}_{3}){c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}({c}_{1}+{c}_{3})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244094 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552 :topic/id #uuid "a92f5097-81a3-51a2-90e4-397763d967f0" :topic/math-academy-id 1909 :topic/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "770d2c3d-fab1-5534-b9f7-4513807d6490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447944} {:answer/id #uuid "c60c30e7-c8d1-56da-a7b1-dc52f59da093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "770d2c3d-fab1-5534-b9f7-4513807d6490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447944} :answer-field/id #uuid "890929b2-27b3-5456-a5f9-abd2dd6daf0a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449012} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e52ae039-5ca2-5c93-a689-46d8b5dd4bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447946} {:answer/id #uuid "08849927-52fb-5b91-8112-4419f09049f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08849927-52fb-5b91-8112-4419f09049f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447947} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7efffcf-b71e-5e32-915d-99527df6ddf3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449013} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01c41aa3-6b8b-5b83-92bb-a4e15d730974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447948} {:answer/id #uuid "7abed29e-4e66-5c0a-a1f1-fc5b1148032b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01c41aa3-6b8b-5b83-92bb-a4e15d730974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447948} :answer-field/id #uuid "c61da28f-b277-5691-8f60-64afce3eb353" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "013a3d9d-c3ab-5357-a425-3057635e56b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-107588" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- **II.** ${{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- **III.** ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},2{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$, any vector of ${R}^{3},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This means that ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}({b}_{1})+{x}_{2}(2{b}_{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+(2{x}_{2}){b}_{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, ${{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}}$ spans ${R}^{2}$ since any linear combination of $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2}(2{b}_{2})\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The set ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},2{c}_{2}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the third vector is a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that the set is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552 :topic/id #uuid "a92f5097-81a3-51a2-90e4-397763d967f0" :topic/math-academy-id 1909 :topic/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68307327-d553-5c0f-9277-b38e2b528f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461491} {:answer/id #uuid "78c1a89a-520f-55bb-aeb2-e65b35d1b2e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68307327-d553-5c0f-9277-b38e2b528f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461491} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a7e9756-5679-5939-b675-05db781b0860" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462499} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02422796-c8f7-540a-9e57-9f2c6bc52ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461493} {:answer/id #uuid "18fccc31-a4b6-5f00-aca3-c8da917759ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02422796-c8f7-540a-9e57-9f2c6bc52ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461493} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac1a6f96-76c2-548b-933c-7e9652bad3d7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462500} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "584b6ea8-930f-5b89-94ba-983ab55cd684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461495} {:answer/id #uuid "9b1bc6a2-d551-55b3-9121-200306d4de9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "584b6ea8-930f-5b89-94ba-983ab55cd684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461495} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec95b7a8-1630-5830-acd6-e28d5986273c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fba7ca1e-918b-5610-87e4-9553b3a87d7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107589" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}$ and ${R}^{2}$, respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n\nII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n\nIII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$, any vector of ${R}^{2},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x\\}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the vector ${b}_{3}$ is in ${R}^{3}$ but it is not a linear combination of  ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ as the set  $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. This means that the set does not span ${R}^{3}$ and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement III is false. The set $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"."}]
